/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  THE COMPONENT BASIS the analysis is FORMULATED ONTO -- and why it is these
  four and not any four.

  The sheet names three masters (Ca, Cl, HCO3).  The components must carry all
  three, and the map from components to masters must be FULL COLUMN RANK, or
  reading the answer back would be a choice of labels rather than a result:

                CO2   CaCO3   CaCl2
        HCO3  [  1      1       0   ]
        Ca    [  0      1       1   ]      m = A n,  rank 3
        Cl    [  0      0       2   ]

  Read the matrix upwards and the inversion is visible: chloride fixes CaCl2,
  what calcium is left fixes CaCO3, what alkalinity is left fixes CO2.  That
  chain is also why CHLORIDE CAN BE THE ADJUSTED SPECIES here -- every mole
  moved into Cl is absorbed by a component that exists, and the two behind it
  follow.  Adjust chloride in a system whose only chloride component is NaCl
  and the reachability check refuses: sodium would have to move with it.

  (The failure mode this rank test exists for is
  flash15_refused_salt_basis_rank: two cations against two anions loses exactly
  (c-1)(a-1) = 1 degree of freedom and the labels stop meaning anything.)

  The reference formulation and the equilibrium model are flash18's, unchanged
  -- an aqueous gamma-phi electrolyte on the carbonate network with Davies
  activities.  This case changes what the INLET may be written as, and nothing
  about the thermodynamics that then reads it.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

recordType    thermophysicalPropertySystem;
schemaVersion 2;

components    ( CO2  CaCO3  CaCl2  water );

equilibrium
{
    formulation electrolyteGammaPhi;

    aqueous
    {
        activityModel { model davies; }
    }

    vapour    { fugacityModel idealGas; }

    volatiles ( CO2  water );
}
